  "attempt": "The exact Collatz inversion gain is localized: it is not a function of word length m, valuation sum S, the valuation multiset, and ordinary inversion count. For every K>=6, A_K=(K,1,1,1,2) and B_K=(2,1,1,K,1) share all four coarse statistics and have inversion count three, but C(A_K)=81+65*2^K and C(B_K)=309+16*2^K. Relative to the same descending word their gains are 38*2^K and 87*2^K-228. At the abstract start n=1, B_K satisfies the affine descent inequality while A_K does not.",
  "bounded_result": {
    "audit_ref": "two_cutoff_localized_variation_directional_audit.collatz"
  },
  "obstruction": "The no-go family is an exact affine valuation-word statement, not a divergent Collatz orbit. Position-weighted gain remains exact, but no theorem forces every natural valuation ray to cross its affine threshold.",
  "candidate_theorem": "NaturalValuationPrefixLocalizedGainCrossesAffineThresholdOnEveryRay",
  "next_experiment": "derive a prefix-localized gain process on natural valuation rays and prove an eventual affine-threshold crossing theorem",
  "claim_boundary": "No Collatz proof and no divergent orbit. One infinite abstract word counterfamily and a natural first-descent collision audit through 100,000.",
  "result": {
